faqs:
  - question: "How do I return a Response object from an Appwrite Function?"
    answer: "In Node.js 18 and later, Bun, and Deno, return a Fetch API `Response`, such as `return new Response('Hello', { status: 200 })`. Appwrite sends its status, headers, and body to the client, and a `ReadableStream` body is streamed. See [Return a response](/docs/products/functions/develop#native-response)."
  - question: "How do I get the Fetch API Request in an Appwrite Function?"
    answer: "Read `context.req.raw`. It returns a `Request` with the same URL, method, headers, and body as `context.req`. You can read its body once, and `context.req.bodyText`, `bodyJson`, and `bodyBinary` keep working after you do. See [Read the request](/docs/products/functions/develop#native-request)."
  - question: "Which runtimes support native Request and Response?"
    answer: "Node.js 18 and later, every Bun version, and every Deno version. The deprecated `node-14.5` and `node-16.0` runtimes don't have the `Request` and `Response` classes, so functions on those runtimes keep using `context.req` and the `context.res` methods."
  - question: "Do I have to change my existing functions?"
    answer: "No. `context.req` and the `context.res` methods work exactly as before. One function can also mix both, for example by reading a form from `context.req.raw` and answering with `context.res.json()`."
  - question: "Does Appwrite have a Hono integration?"
    answer: "No integration is needed. Hono's `app.fetch()` takes a `Request` and returns a `Response`, so a function that exports `async ({ req }) => app.fetch(req.raw)` runs a Hono app. Any other framework that exposes its app as a function from `Request` to `Response` works the same way."
  - question: "How do I use log() and the Appwrite API key inside Hono routes?"
    answer: "Appwrite headers such as `x-appwrite-key` and `x-appwrite-user-id` are on the request, so a route reads them with `c.req.header()`. To log, pass the function context to Hono with `app.fetch(req.raw, context)` and call `c.env.log()` inside a route."
---

Most JavaScript runtimes and frameworks now handle HTTP requests the same way. A handler takes a [`Request`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Request) and returns a [`Response`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response). `Bun.serve()`, `Deno.serve()`, and Cloudflare Workers work that way, and so do frameworks built on the same classes, such as Hono.

Appwrite Functions had their own shape. `context.req` held the parsed request, and you built the answer with `context.res.text()` or `context.res.json()`. That works for a single endpoint, but code written for the Fetch API needed an adapter before it could run in a function. Even reading a multipart form meant wrapping `req.bodyBinary` in a temporary `Response` just to call `formData()` on it.

Today, we are announcing **native Request and Response support in JavaScript functions**. In Node.js 18 and later, Bun, and Deno, `context.req.raw` gives you the incoming request as a Fetch API `Request`, and your function can return a `Response` instead of calling a `context.res` method.

# What native Request and Response give you

- **The standard request methods.** `context.req.raw` is a real `Request`, so `formData()`, `json()`, `text()`, `arrayBuffer()`, and `blob()` all work on it, including for multipart uploads.
- **Responses you build yourself.** Return `new Response(body, { status, headers })` and Appwrite sends it to the client as written.
- **Responses built by other libraries.** If a library returns a `Response`, your function can return it as is. A `ReadableStream` body is streamed to the client, the same way as [`res.sendStream()`](/blog/post/announcing-streaming-responses).
- **Frameworks built on the Fetch API.** A framework whose app takes a `Request` and returns a `Response`, such as Hono, now runs in a function without an adapter.
- **Nothing to migrate.** `context.req` and `context.res` work as before, and a function can use both.

# Use Request and Response in Node.js, Bun, and Deno

This function reads a form from `context.req.raw` and answers with a `Response`. It uses only the classes built into the runtime, with no Appwrite helpers.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
export default async ({ req }) => {
    if (req.method !== 'POST') {
        return new Response('Send the form with POST', { status: 405 });
    }

    const form = await req.raw.formData();
    const name = form.get('name');

    return new Response(`Hello, ${name}!`, {
        headers: { 'content-type': 'text/plain' }
    });
};
```
```bun
export default async ({ req }) => {
    if (req.method !== 'POST') {
        return new Response('Send the form with POST', { status: 405 });
    }

    const form = await req.raw.formData();
    const name = form.get('name');

    return new Response(`Hello, ${name}!`, {
        headers: { 'content-type': 'text/plain' }
    });
};
```
```deno
export default async ({ req }: any) => {
    if (req.method !== "POST") {
        return new Response("Send the form with POST", { status: 405 });
    }

    const form = await req.raw.formData();
    const name = form.get("name");

    return new Response(`Hello, ${name}!`, {
        headers: { "content-type": "text/plain" }
    });
};
```
{% /multicode %}

`context.req.raw` has the same URL, method, headers, and body as `context.req`, including Appwrite headers such as `x-appwrite-key` and `x-appwrite-user-id`. You can read its body once, like any `Request`, and `req.bodyText`, `req.bodyJson`, and `req.bodyBinary` keep working after you do. `formData()` parses URL-encoded HTML forms and multipart uploads, and `form.get('file')` returns a `File` with its name, type, and contents.

Until now, reading a multipart body meant building a `Response` around the raw bytes, as the [email trigger](/docs/products/functions/email) examples do. With `context.req.raw`, it's one call:

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #form-after title="After" %}

```server-nodejs
const form = await req.raw.formData();
```

{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #form-before title="Before" %}

```server-nodejs
const form = await new Response(req.bodyBinary, {
    headers: { 'content-type': req.headers['content-type'] }
}).formData();
```

{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

# Return the Response a library builds

Some libraries finish their work by building a `Response`. The [Vercel AI SDK](https://ai-sdk.dev) is one. In the [streaming announcement](/blog/post/announcing-streaming-responses), we passed its text stream to `res.sendStream()`. Now the function can return the SDK's own response:

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
import { streamText } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

export default async ({ req }) => {
    const result = streamText({
        model: openai('gpt-5-mini'),
        prompt: req.bodyJson.prompt
    });

    return result.toTextStreamResponse();
};
```
```bun
import { streamText } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

export default async ({ req }) => {
    const result = streamText({
        model: openai('gpt-5-mini'),
        prompt: req.bodyJson.prompt
    });

    return result.toTextStreamResponse();
};
```
```deno
import { streamText } from "npm:ai";
import { openai } from "npm:@ai-sdk/openai";

export default async ({ req }: any) => {
    const result = streamText({
        model: openai("gpt-5-mini"),
        prompt: req.bodyJson.prompt
    });

    return result.toTextStreamResponse();
};
```
{% /multicode %}

Its body is a `ReadableStream`, so the reply reaches the browser as the model writes it. The same streaming rules apply as for `sendStream()`. Clients see the chunks when they call the function through its domain, and the stream ends at the function's timeout or after 60 seconds, whichever comes first.

# Run Fetch API frameworks such as Hono

Appwrite doesn't ship an integration for any particular framework, and it doesn't need one. A framework built on the Fetch API exposes your app as a single function that takes a `Request` and returns a `Response`. That's the same shape `Bun.serve()` and `Deno.serve()` call. Until now, running one of these frameworks in a function meant writing an adapter that turned `context.req` into a `Request` and copied the `Response` back into a `context.res` call. Now you pass `context.req.raw` in and return what comes out:

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #framework-after title="After" %}

```server-nodejs
export default async ({ req }) => app.fetch(req.raw);
```

{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #framework-before title="Before" %}

```server-nodejs
export default async ({ req, res }) => {
    const request = new Request(req.url, {
        method: req.method,
        headers: req.headers,
        body: ['GET', 'HEAD'].includes(req.method) ? undefined : req.bodyBinary
    });

    const response = await app.fetch(request);
    const body = Buffer.from(await response.arrayBuffer());

    return res.binary(body, response.status, Object.fromEntries(response.headers));
};
```

{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

[Hono](https://hono.dev) is one of these frameworks. Its `app.fetch()` method is that single function, so the whole integration is the last line of this function:

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
import { Hono } from 'hono';

const app = new Hono();

app.get('/', (c) => c.text('Hello from Hono!'));

app.get('/me', (c) => {
    const userId = c.req.header('x-appwrite-user-id');
    if (!userId) {
        return c.json({ message: 'Sign in first' }, 401);
    }

    return c.json({ userId });
});

export default async ({ req }) => app.fetch(req.raw);
```
```bun
import { Hono } from 'hono';

const app = new Hono();

app.get('/', (c) => c.text('Hello from Hono!'));

app.get('/me', (c) => {
    const userId = c.req.header('x-appwrite-user-id');
    if (!userId) {
        return c.json({ message: 'Sign in first' }, 401);
    }

    return c.json({ userId });
});

export default async ({ req }) => app.fetch(req.raw);
```
```deno
import { Hono } from "npm:hono";

const app = new Hono();

app.get("/", (c) => c.text("Hello from Hono!"));

app.get("/me", (c) => {
    const userId = c.req.header("x-appwrite-user-id");
    if (!userId) {
        return c.json({ message: "Sign in first" }, 401);
    }

    return c.json({ userId });
});

export default async ({ req }: any) => app.fetch(req.raw);
```
{% /multicode %}

Add `hono` to your `package.json` in Node.js and Bun. Deno imports it with an `npm:` specifier. A few details make this fit the rest of Appwrite Functions:

- **Routes match the request path.** A request to the function's [domain](/docs/products/functions/domains) uses the path in its URL. An [execution](/docs/products/functions/execute) created from an SDK uses the path you pass when you create it, so a signed-in user can call `/me` from your app.
- **Appwrite headers come along.** `x-appwrite-key`, `x-appwrite-user-id`, `x-appwrite-trigger`, and the other [function headers](/docs/products/functions/develop#headers) are on the `Request`, so routes read them with `c.req.header()`.
- **Logging still works.** Pass the function context as Hono's environment with `app.fetch(req.raw, context)`, then call `c.env.log()` or `c.env.error()` inside a route.

Any other framework that exposes its app as a function from `Request` to `Response` works the same way.

# When to use native Request and Response

Reach for `context.req.raw` and `Response` when:

- You need `formData()` or another standard body parser.
- A library you use takes a `Request` or returns a `Response`.
- You're moving a handler from `Bun.serve()`, `Deno.serve()`, or Cloudflare Workers and want to keep its `Request` in, `Response` out shape.
- One function serves several routes and you want a framework such as Hono instead of a hand-written `switch` on `req.path`.

Stay with `context.req` and `context.res` when the function is a single endpoint. `req.bodyJson` and `res.json()` are shorter than parsing and building the objects yourself. The `context.res` methods also look the same in every Appwrite runtime, which helps if your team writes functions in more than one language.

One limit to know about. If a returned `Response` sets more than one cookie, use `node-20.0` or later, Bun, or Deno. Earlier Node.js versions join the cookies into a single `set-cookie` header, and browsers don't split it back into separate cookies.

# Getting started with native Request and Response

Native Request and Response support is available on Appwrite Cloud today in the Node.js 18 and later, Bun, and Deno runtimes. Pick one of those runtimes, read the request from `req.raw`, and return a `Response` when that's simpler than a `context.res` method.

